    "b-sks-lifecycle-intro": {
      "id": "b-sks-lifecycle-intro",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Confidence is a judgment about a specific claim, not a substitute for evidence. A claim is a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "fact",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " row: "
        },
        {
          "insert": "value",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " is the claim, "
        },
        {
          "insert": "rationale",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " is the librarian's argument for it, "
        },
        {
          "insert": "confidence",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " (0..1) says how sure we are, and the fact's "
        },
        {
          "insert": "evidence",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " rows carry the citations the argument stands on — each a pointer into a knowledge source with a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "why",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". The librarian is the only writer of facts and evidence; a changed claim overwrites its live row in place, replacing its evidence rows wholesale. This page covers how confidence is assigned and revised, the gates every fact write passes, and what happens to facts and evidence as the target underneath them drifts, is reconciled, or fails to integrate."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-lifecycle-resolution": {
      "id": "b-sks-lifecycle-resolution",
      "type": "heading",
