    "b-sks-lifecycle-revisions": {
      "id": "b-sks-lifecycle-revisions",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Drift, Reconciliation, and Integration Outcomes"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-lifecycle-revisions-p": {
      "id": "b-sks-lifecycle-revisions-p",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Facts hang off subjects, and the report-derived subjects move. Reconciliation diffs each new report revision against the stored "
        },
        {
          "insert": "target",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " rows by "
        },
        {
          "insert": "stable_key",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "address",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", and moves "
        },
        {
          "insert": "identity_status",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " only through explicit rules — "
        },
        {
          "insert": "current",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "moved",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "unresolved",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "retired",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". A rename within a unit — one vanished key and one new key at the same address and kind, paired 1:1 — is continuity, not loss: the old row becomes "
        },
        {
          "insert": "moved",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " with "
        },
        {
          "insert": "moved_to_id",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " naming the successor, and its facts, evidence, and links are re-pointed at the successor in the same transaction. A vanished item is never deleted and never reattached to a guessed symbol. A changed "
        },
        {
          "insert": "target_status.content_hash",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " alone is status, not identity: it never moves "
        },
        {
          "insert": "identity_status",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-lifecycle-drift-facts-p": {
      "id": "b-sks-lifecycle-drift-facts-p",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Code drift is caught by the deterministic flagger, not by reconciliation: at the checkout head it re-resolves each "
        },
        {
          "insert": "code://",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " citation's path and span, recomputes the digest, and classifies it unchanged, drifted, or unresolvable — read-only, no model involved. Archival sources and attempt rows are immutable once ingested, so their locators cannot drift. The flagger runs inside the librarian's "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pr_imported",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "run_closed",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " contexts, which carry per touched subject the compact drift report and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "renamed_from",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " — the stable keys of rows whose "
        },
        {
          "insert": "moved_to_id",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " points at it, so a fact re-pointed by rename continuity keeps its lineage. The consumer gate then re-runs the flagger after apply: clean completes the pass; drift left on the first attempt releases the task for one bounded retry; drift left after the retry completes with the warning recorded in the pass artifact — see "
        },
        {
          "insert": "Drift Gate",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "doc",
              "path": "10-system-design/40-knowledge/40-librarian-pathways/50-drift-gate",
              "label": "Drift Gate"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". The flag never silently edits a fact — a drop or a re-cite is an ordinary librarian overwrite (which replaces the fact's evidence rows wholesale) or a clear (which deletes the fact and its evidence rows together). Drift itself never deletes knowledge: facts on a retired or unresolved target stay stored."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-lifecycle-regressions-p": {
      "id": "b-sks-lifecycle-regressions-p",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Integration outcomes live on the run row, not in knowledge. When a run's diff fails to integrate at the boundary, the run-closed writer records "
        },
        {
          "insert": "integration",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " "
        },
        {
          "insert": "conflicted",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "integration_detail",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " (status, disposition, conflict paths, failure reasons, resolved_at) on the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "worker_run",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " row, derived deterministically from run state; the next worker sees the conflicted run, with its conflict paths, on the card. No "
        },
        {
          "insert": "event",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " row is written — the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "regression",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " kind is retired — and reconciliation records nothing in the ledger. The facts on the target do not change because the score did — the librarian revises a fact only when the claim about meaning changes. A later fact leans on a failed integration by citing the run through an "
        },
